#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/15-pandas-select-aggregate-join.md:1009
msgid "SAS idioms in pandas"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/15-pandas-select-aggregate-join.md:1011
msgid "**`FIRST.` and `LAST.`** - the first or last row of each `BY` group. Sort, then keep the first row per key:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/15-pandas-select-aggregate-join.md:1025
msgid "`groupby(\"client_id\").head(1)` does the same, and `.head(2)` keeps the first two of each group - not possible with `FIRST.` alone. `keep=\"last\"` is `LAST.`."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/15-pandas-select-aggregate-join.md:1028
msgid "**`NODUPKEY`** - `drop_duplicates(subset=[...])` (session 14); `duplicated(keep=False)` marks **all** copies, to look at them:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/15-pandas-select-aggregate-join.md:1040
msgid "**`LAG` and `RETAIN` by group** - the value of the previous row of the same client, and a running total that restarts for each client:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/15-pandas-select-aggregate-join.md:1054
msgid "`shift(1)` inside the group gives `NaN` on the first row of each client - exactly what `LAG` with `IF FIRST.client_id THEN prev = .` does, without the trap of `LAG` in a condition."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/15-pandas-select-aggregate-join.md:1058
msgid "**A row counter per group** - `cumcount` (`IF FIRST. THEN n = 0; n + 1;`):"
msgstr ""

